      "dom_id": "question-73776",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x+4?$\n\nI. $x=4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder",
      "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & ={4}^{2}+5(4)+4 \\\\  & =16+20+4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
